# 12. ENTRYPOINT
# Main executable of the container
ENTRYPOINT ["nginx"]

What is CMD?
CMD ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]

It says: When the container starts, run this command.

So: docker run myapp
➡️ Docker runs:
java -jar app.jar
What does "override" mean?

Suppose your Dockerfile says:
CMD ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]

But when starting the container, you say: docker run myapp bash

Docker says: "Okay, instead of the default CMD, I'll run bash."

So:
Dockerfile:
CMD → java -jar app.jar
              ↓
        DEFAULT command

docker run myapp bash
              ↓
        REPLACE CMD
              ↓
             bash
That's all "easy to override" means.


Suppose Dockerfile has:
CMD ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]
Case 1: No command given
docker run myapp
Docker uses the default CMD:

CMD
 ↓
java -jar app.jar
 ↓
Java application starts
Case 2: You give bash
docker run -it myapp bash

Now bash replaces the CMD:

CMD: java -jar app.jar
          ↓
       REPLACED
          ↓
       bash runs

So Java does NOT start.

You are now inside the container's shell: root@container:/app#
If you manually type: java -jar app.jar
then Java starts.



CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]
means: When the container starts, run Nginx in the foreground.
Break it down:
"nginx"          → program to run
"-g"             → Nginx option for global configuration
"daemon off;"    → tell Nginx NOT to run in the background

So Docker effectively runs:
nginx -g "daemon off;"
Why daemon off;?
Normally Nginx tries to run as a background process (daemon).
But a Docker container needs its main process to keep running in the foreground.

If Nginx goes into the background:
Container
└── main process finishes
       ↓
Container stops ❌

With: daemon off;
Nginx stays in the foreground:

Container
└── nginx (main process)
       ↓
Container keeps running ✅
Why is this important?
Docker considers the container's main process (PID 1) to be what keeps the container alive.
So: CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]
means: Start Nginx as the main foreground process so the container stays running.
----------------------------
ENTRYPOINT is used when you want a command to be the main program of the container and you don't normally want docker run arguments to replace it.

Example
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar"]
CMD ["app.jar"]

Why use ENTRYPOINT?
Suppose your container is specifically designed to run your Java application:

ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar"]
CMD ["app.jar"]

You are saying:
"This container's main job is to run Java. You can change the JAR/arguments, but Java is the main executable."

Suppose your Dockerfile is:
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar"]
CMD ["app.jar"]

Now you run: docker run -it myimage bash
What happens? bash does not replace the ENTRYPOINT.
Docker treats bash as an argument to the ENTRYPOINT:
ENTRYPOINT          argument
    ↓                  ↓
java -jar          bash
        ↓
java -jar bash

So Docker tries to run: java -jar bash
It does NOT open a bash shell.

Simple rule
CMD only:
docker run image bash
        ↓
bash replaces CMD
ENTRYPOINT + CMD:
docker run image bash
        ↓
bash becomes an argument to ENTRYPOINT
        ↓
java -jar bash

If you actually want to override the ENTRYPOINT and run bash, Docker provides:
docker run -it --entrypoint bash myimage

Now:
--entrypoint bash
       ↓
ENTRYPOINT is replaced
       ↓
bash runs

So --entrypoint is how you override an ENTRYPOINT.


Yes, you can put everything in ENTRYPOINT.
For example: ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]

Then: docker run myimage
runs: java -jar app.jar
So why do people use:
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar"]
CMD ["app.jar"]

instead?
Because CMD gives you a default argument that can be changed.

With:
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar"]
CMD ["app.jar"]

normally: docker run myimage
java -jar app.jar

But you can give another JAR: docker run myimage test.jar
java -jar test.jar

The ENTRYPOINT stays fixed: java -jar
and CMD is replaced: app.jar → test.jar

If everything is in ENTRYPOINT
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]

Then: docker run myimage test.jar
does not replace app.jar.
It becomes an additional argument:
java -jar app.jar test.jar
So the reason is flexibility
ENTRYPOINT = What must always run
CMD        = Default arguments


Ah, you mean whether you can write: ENTRYPOINT ["java -jar"]
instead of: ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar"]

No, not in the JSON/exec form. ❌
Why?

In: ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar"]
Docker sees two separate items:

"java"  → executable/command
"-jar"  → argument

So it runs: java -jar
But in: ENTRYPOINT ["java -jar"]
Docker sees one item: "java -jar"

It tries to find an executable literally named: java -jar
which doesn't exist. ❌

But you can write it using shell form
This is valid: ENTRYPOINT java -jar app.jar
Here Docker uses shell-style execution, so java -jar app.jar is treated as a command line.














  
